1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauno_Jokipii '''Mauno Jokipii'''] (21 August 1924 – 2 January 2007) was a Finnish professor at the University of Jyväskylä in history specializing in World War II. He was a thorough 
2 CONC investigator and a prolific author. Among his works were studies of the local history of Jyväskylä and the university and historical province of Satakunta.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jokipii was born''' in Helsinki. Jokipii attended University of Helsinki from 1952 to 1959, and began publishing almost immediately (1954). He received his doctorate in Philosophy in 1957. He was Dean of the History-Linguistics
2 CONC  department from 1960 to 1966.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Although some of''' his early works had been noted previously, Jokipii sprung on the world scene with his 900-page study of the Finnish SS battalion in World War II. This study established a new level of detail in World War II 
2 CONC military history. Jokipii's massive work The Continuation War: An Investigation of German-Finnish Military Collaboration 1940-1941 firmly established him as a world scholar and moved the debate about German Finnish relations durin
2 CONC g World War II to a new level.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In the recent''' years the academic integrity of his work has been fashionably questioned, in particular his work on the Finnish SS battalion has been accused by younger, politically correct researchers including André Swanströ
2 CONC m, Marko Tikka, and Oula Silvennoinen of politically motivated whitewashing in order to conceal war crimes committed by the battalion. 
